<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step-1: Textual subspace decomposition.</span> 
To address the over-reliance of the [EOT] token on generic semantic attributes, we start by disentangling the text representations into two complementary subspaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, for an <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer text encoder, let <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the attention weights between the [EOT] token and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> subword tokens at layer <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. We compute layer-wise importance <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{l}=\|\mathbf{h}^{l}_{\text{eot}}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>eot</mtext><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{l}=\|\mathbf{h}^{l}_{\text{eot}}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to weight each layer’s contribution. <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the activation magnitude of the [EOT] token’s hidden state <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{l}_{\text{eot}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>eot</mtext><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{l}_{\text{eot}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it has been observed that the feature magnitude is directly related to its importance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Therefore, the aggregated attention for the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th token is computed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}=\frac{\sum_{l=1}^{L}\gamma_{l}\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}}{\sum_{l=1}^{L}\gamma_{l}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}=\frac{\sum_{l=1}^{L}\gamma_{l}\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}}{\sum_{l=1}^{L}\gamma_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the total attention allocated to the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th token across all encoder layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, given <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can decompose textual features into two complementary subspaces with the threshold <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">General Attribute Subspace:</span> Tokens with high attention values (<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}\geq\tau_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}\geq\tau_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) contribute to the representation of generic concepts <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, encoding coarse-grained attributes (e.g., object categories like “apparel" or “animal") that facilitate cross-modal alignment due to their strong correlation with visual primitives.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discriminative Attribute Subspace:</span> Tokens with low attention values (<math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{k}&lt;\tau_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{k}&lt;\tau_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) form the features of discriminative concepts <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, capturing more detailed characteristics (e.g., “striped texture" or “running action") essential for precise visual comprehension but often overlooked during the attention process.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step-2: Adaptive semantic modulation.</span> 
With the decoupled subspaces for general and discriminative attributes respectively, we can alleviate the over-reliance on the general attributes with an adaptive token masking strategy, which can be formulated as:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{a}}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left[(1-m_{i})\cdot\mathbf{t}_{g}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>a</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{a}}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left[(1-m_{i})\cdot\mathbf{t}_{g}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">In Eq. (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In 4.3. Discriminative Concept Calibrator ‣ 4. Method ‣ CalibCLIP: Contextual Calibration of Dominant Semantics for Text-Driven Image Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), the features of the general attribute tokens <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are scaled by a modulation coefficient <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the attenuated representation <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the coefficient as <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}=\frac{|\mathcal{D}|}{|\mathcal{G}+\mathcal{D}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}=\frac{|\mathcal{D}|}{|\mathcal{G}+\mathcal{D}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{G}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{G}|</annotation></semantics></math> and<math id="S4.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{D}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{D}|</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of general and discriminative attributes.
Intuitively, when text descriptions contain abundant discriminative details (i.e., <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{D}|\gg|\mathcal{G}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{D}|\gg|\mathcal{G}|</annotation></semantics></math>), the mask values tend toward <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>, suppressing <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> while enhancing <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> to prioritize fine-grained distinctions.
Conversely, when discriminative cues are scarce (i.e., <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{G}|\gg|\mathcal{D}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{G}|\gg|\mathcal{D}|</annotation></semantics></math>), the mask values decay toward <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, preserving <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to maintain semantic stability and ensure robust cross-modal alignment under sparse textual descriptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step-3: Inference with discriminative similarity.</span> 
The cross-modal retrieval result is typically predicted by selecting the sample pair with maximal similarity <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}(\mathbf{t}_{\text{eot}},\mathbf{v}_{\text{cls}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mtext>eot</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mtext>cls</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}(\mathbf{t}_{\text{eot}},\mathbf{v}_{\text{cls}})</annotation></semantics></math> between the text’s terminal [EOT] token <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{\text{eot}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mtext>eot</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{\text{eot}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the image’s [CLS] token <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{\text{cls}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mtext>cls</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{\text{cls}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koley et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
While the modulated feature <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> (from Step-2) alleviates contextual dominance bias, the conventional similarity measurement <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}(\mathbf{t}_{\text{eot}},\mathbf{v}_{\text{cls}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mtext>eot</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mtext>cls</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}(\mathbf{t}_{\text{eot}},\mathbf{v}_{\text{cls}})</annotation></semantics></math> remains suboptimal for capturing fine-grained discrepancies. This limitation arises because the [EOT] token inherently encapsulates global textual semantics, potentially obscuring nuanced discriminative features that reside in intermediate token interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate this limitation, we establish a new token with enhanced discriminative cues to complement the [EOT] token. First, we introduce a new token <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, concatenated with the modulated feature <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and discriminative feature <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, to be processed through a self-attention layer:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{\text{r}},\_,\_=\text{Self-Attention}([\mathbf{r};\mathbf{t}_{a};\mathbf{t}_{d}])" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mtext>r</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>_</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>_</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Self-Attention</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{\text{r}},\_,\_=\text{Self-Attention}([\mathbf{r};\mathbf{t}_{a};\mathbf{t}_{d}])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{\text{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mtext>r</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{\text{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the self-attention output of the learnable token <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, a cross-attention layer is adopted to generate <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\hat{t}}_{\text{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>r</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\hat{t}}_{\text{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{\text{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mtext>r</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{\text{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the query, and the key and value being the concatenation of <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this context, <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\hat{t}}_{\text{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>r</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\hat{t}}_{\text{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> incorporates essential fine-grained information from the modulated feature <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and discriminative feature <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> to enhance the differentiation between similar concepts.</p>
</div>
